    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: '**DMD Disease Course and Current Stage**:\n- DMD follows a predictable trajectory: early ambulatory (diagnosis to ~7 years), late ambulatory (7-12 years), early non-ambulatory (12-15 years), late non-ambulatory (>15 years)\n- This patient is in the **late ambulatory to early non-ambulatory transition** — a critical and emotionally charged period\n- Loss of ambulation typically occurs between ages 9-13 (deflazacort extends this by 2-3 years on average)\n- His declining 6-Minute Walk Test and increasing falls indicate this transition is imminent\n\n**Corticosteroid Management**:\n- **Deflazacort 0.9 mg/kg/day** is standard of care — prolongs ambulation, delays scoliosis, preserves pulmonary and cardiac function\n- Side effects to monitor: weight gain (deflazacort has less weight gain than prednisone), cataracts (annual ophthalmology screening), growth deceleration, vertebral compression fractures, adrenal suppression, behavioral changes, glucose intolerance\n- **Never abruptly discontinue** — risk of adrenal crisis. Stress-dose steroids needed for illness or surgery.\n- Continue corticosteroids into non-ambulatory phase — ongoing benefits for cardiac and pulmonary function\n\n**Cardiac Monitoring**:\n- Cardiomyopathy is universal in DMD, typically developing by age 10-15\n- Current LVEF 58% is at the lower end of normal — requires close monitoring\n- **Standard**: Echocardiogram and ECG every 12 months (every 6 months after age 10 or if LVEF <55%)\n- **Cardiac MRI**: More sensitive than echocardiogram for early fibrosis. Consider if echo shows borderline function.\n- **ACE inhibitor or ARB**: Initiate prophylactically by age 10 or at first sign of dysfunction (lisinopril or losartan). This patient should be started on prophylactic cardiac protection now.\n- **Beta-blocker**: Add if LVEF declines below 55%\n\n**Pulmonary Monitoring**:\n- FVC 72% predicted indicates early restrictive lung disease\n- **Standard**: Spirometry every 6 months once FVC <80% predicted\n- **Cough assessment**: Peak cough flow <270 L/min indicates inadequate cough and need for assisted cough device\n- **Sleep study**: Obtain when FVC <50% or if symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation (morning headaches, daytime somnolence, vivid dreams)\n- **Nocturnal BiPAP**: Initiate when FVC <50% predicted, sleep study shows hypoventilation, or daytime hypercapnia develops\n- **Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (CoughAssist)**: Prescribe when peak cough flow <270 L/min or during respiratory illnesses\n\n**Musculoskeletal Assessment**:\n- **Contractures**: Assess ankle dorsiflexion (equinus contractures common), knee flexion contractures, hip flexion contractures, IT band tightness\n- **Scoliosis**: Monitor closely during non-ambulatory transition. Progression is rapid without corticosteroids. Surgical spinal fusion considered when Cobb angle >20-30 degrees and before FVC drops below 30-40% predicted.\n- **Fractures**: Low bone density from corticosteroids, immobility, and vitamin D deficiency. Vertebral compression fractures may be asymptomatic. Consider lateral spine radiograph and DXA scan.\n- **Bone health**: Vitamin D supplementation (target 25-OH vitamin D >30 ng/mL), calcium supplementation, weight-bearing as tolerated\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- North Star Ambulatory Assessment (NSAA): standardized functional scale for ambulatory DMD\n- Brooke Upper Extremity Scale and Vignos Lower Extremity Scale for longitudinal monitoring\n- Performance of Upper Limb (PUL): Important as upper extremity function becomes primary concern in non-ambulatory phase',
