      title: '+ Discuss the evidence-based approach to power wheelchair prescription in DMD, including standing frame use and the monitoring schedule during the non-ambulatory transition.',
      content: '**Power Wheelchair Prescription — Timing and Approach**:\n\n**When to Introduce a Power Wheelchair**:\n- The wheelchair should be framed as a tool for independence, NOT as "giving up"\n- Evidence supports early introduction — before complete loss of ambulation — so the child can learn to operate it while still ambulatory\n- This patient\'s indicators for immediate wheelchair prescription: 6-Minute Walk Test <300 meters and declining, frequent falls (safety issue), functional mobility limiting school and social participation, declining NSAA score\n- **Dual mobility approach**: Use the power wheelchair for community and school distances while encouraging walking for short household distances as long as safe and not exhausting\n\n**Power Wheelchair Specifications for DMD**:\n- **Power tilt-in-space**: Essential for pressure redistribution, respiratory support, and positioning as the disease progresses\n- **Power recline**: For pressure relief and comfort\n- **Elevating legrests**: For edema management and contracture positioning\n- **Seat elevator**: Allows the child to reach counters, talk at eye level with peers, and access school desks — critical for psychosocial development\n- **Custom seating**: Contoured seat and back cushions, lateral trunk supports (will become critical as scoliosis develops), headrest (will need as neck flexor weakness progresses)\n- **Expandable frame**: The chair must accommodate growth and disease progression for 3-5 years. Consider a frame that can be reconfigured as needs change.\n- **Joystick control**: Standard hand joystick initially. Plan for alternative drive controls (mini-joystick, head array, sip-and-puff) as upper extremity weakness progresses in later stages.\n- **Mounting hardware**: For tablet/phone, ventilator tray (future), communication device if needed\n- **Environmental controls**: Consider integration with smart home technology (voice-activated devices)\n\n**Standing Frame**:\n- Prescribe a supine or prone standing frame for daily use (45-60 minutes daily)\n- Benefits: bone density preservation, contracture management, bowel and bladder function, psychological well-being\n- May need a sit-to-stand frame or a power standing wheelchair if the family can obtain one\n- Continue as long as the patient can tolerate it, typically into the mid-teens\n\n**Monitoring Schedule During Non-Ambulatory Transition**:\n\n| Assessment | Frequency |\n|---|---|\n| PM&R/Neuromuscular clinic | Every 3-4 months |\n| Pulmonary function tests | Every 6 months |\n| Echocardiogram + ECG | Every 6-12 months (every 6 months if LVEF <55%) |\n| Cardiac MRI | Every 2 years or if echo concerning |\n| Spine radiograph (scoliosis) | Every 6-12 months in non-ambulatory phase |\n| DXA scan | Every 1-2 years |\n| Ophthalmology (cataracts) | Annually |\n| Endocrine (growth, glucose, adrenal) | Annually |\n| Sleep study | When FVC <50% or symptoms present |\n| Lateral spine radiograph | If back pain (vertebral fractures) |\n| Vitamin D level | Every 6-12 months |\n\n**Respiratory Interventions to Initiate Now**:\n- Peak cough flow is 240 L/min (<270 threshold): Prescribe mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (CoughAssist) for home use\n- Train the family in assisted cough techniques (manual and device-assisted)\n- Lung volume recruitment (breath stacking) with a manual resuscitation bag — daily practice to maintain chest wall compliance\n- Influenza and pneumococcal vaccinations annually\n- Aggressive treatment of respiratory infections — early antibiotic therapy, chest physiotherapy, CoughAssist use with illness\n\n**Cardiac Intervention**:\n- Start ACE inhibitor (lisinopril 2.5-5 mg daily) or ARB (losartan) now — evidence supports prophylactic cardioprotection even with normal LVEF in DMD after age 10\n- Monitor blood pressure and renal function after initiation',
