training: Family must be proficient in device use. Settings: +40/-40 cmH2O, 5 cycles, 3 sets with rest between. Use during respiratory infections and daily for secretion management.\n- Airway clearance techniques: High-frequency chest wall oscillation vest can be added if secretion management becomes difficult\n\n**Cardiac Management**:\n- Initiate ACE inhibitor or ARB now\n- If LVEF declines below 55%: Add beta-blocker (carvedilol)\n- If LVEF declines below 45%: Consider eplerenone (aldosterone antagonist), cardiology-guided advanced heart failure management\n- Avoid dehydration — cardiomyopathy patients are preload-dependent\n- Stress-dose steroids for any illness or procedure (adrenal suppression from chronic deflazacort)\n\n**Emerging Therapies**:\n- **Exon-skipping therapies**: Eteplirsen (exon 51), golodirsen (exon 53), viltolarsen (exon 53), casimersen (exon 45). This patient has an exon 45-50 deletion — check eligibility for exon-skipping agents (exon 51 skipping would be needed for a 45-50 deletion to restore reading frame)\n- **Gene therapy**: Micro-dystrophin gene therapy (delandistrogene moxeparvovec/Elevidys) — FDA approved with accelerated approval for DMD ages 4-5, trials ongoing for older children. Discuss with neurology.\n- **Cardiac gene therapy**: In clinical trials for DMD cardiomyopathy\n- These therapies aim to slow progression, not cure, and work best when started early. The PM&R physician should ensure families are connected to a comprehensive DMD center for access to trials.\n\n**Nutrition**:\n- Dietitian consultation: Balance adequate nutrition for growth with weight management (excess weight accelerates functional decline and complicates wheelchair seating)\n- Monitor for corticosteroid-related metabolic effects: glucose intolerance, dyslipidemia\n- Calcium 1000-1300 mg/day + Vitamin D 1000-2000 IU/day for bone health',
