      title: '+ Discuss DMD-specific resources, school accommodations, genetic counseling considerations, and long-term transition planning.',
      content: '**DMD-Specific Resources**:\n- **Muscular Dystrophy Association (MDA)**: Care center network, summer camps, equipment loan programs, advocacy. MDA Care Centers provide multidisciplinary DMD clinics with neurology, PM&R, pulmonology, cardiology, PT/OT, social work in one visit.\n- **Parent Project Muscular Dystrophy (PPMD)**: Family support, research advocacy, educational webinars, annual conference connecting families with leading researchers\n- **CureDuchenne**: Research funding, patient registry, care guidelines\n- **Team Gleason (Steve Gleason Foundation)**: Technology (communication devices, eye-tracking, smart home) and adventure programs for neuromuscular diseases\n- **Jett Foundation**: Financial assistance, programs for boys with DMD\n\n**School Accommodations (IEP/504 Plan)**:\n- **Physical access**: Elevator access, accessible classroom and bathroom, wheelchair-accessible desk, permission to use power wheelchair throughout school\n- **Adapted PE**: Modified physical education with appropriate activities (swimming, adapted sports, light activities). Avoid eccentric exercise and contact sports.\n- **Fatigue management**: Extra time between classes, reduced homework if fatigue impacts completion, option for rest periods\n- **Writing support**: As upper extremity weakness progresses, typing may replace handwriting. Voice-to-text software, extended time for written assignments.\n- **Emergency evacuation plan**: Specific plan for evacuating a wheelchair-dependent student during fire or emergency\n- **Health plan**: Medication administration at school (deflazacort timing), CoughAssist access, trained aide for respiratory emergencies\n- **Psychosocial support**: Regular access to school counselor, anti-bullying protocols, peer education about DMD (with the student\'s permission)\n\n**Genetic Counseling**:\n- **For the mother**: She is a confirmed carrier. Risk of cardiomyopathy in female carriers — echocardiogram recommended for all female carriers. May benefit from genetic counselor to discuss implications for future pregnancies.\n- **For the sister**: She has a 50% chance of being a carrier. Genetic testing can be offered when she is old enough to consent (typically adolescence, though practice varies). Must be handled sensitively.\n- **For the patient\'s future**: DMD males are typically infertile, but with advancing medical therapies and longer survival, fertility counseling may become relevant. Sperm banking can be discussed in adolescence if appropriate.\n- **Family planning for the parents**: If they desire more children, preimplantation genetic diagnosis or prenatal testing is available\n\n**Long-Term Transition Planning**:\n- **Life expectancy**: With modern care (corticosteroids, cardiac management, non-invasive ventilation), survival now extends into the 30s and beyond for many patients\n- **Education and vocation**: Many DMD patients complete college and pursue careers in fields compatible with physical limitations. Encourage academic excellence and career exploration.\n- **Independent living**: Increasingly possible with personal care assistants, smart home technology, and power wheelchair with environmental controls\n- **Transition to adult care**: Begin planning at age 14-16. Identify adult neuromuscular, cardiology, pulmonology, and PM&R providers. Transfer medical records and care summaries.\n- **Guardianship and decision-making**: DMD does not typically affect cognition (though some patients have learning differences related to dystrophin isoforms in the brain). Full decision-making capacity is expected.\n- **Advance care planning**: Should be discussed in late adolescence/early adulthood. Preferences for mechanical ventilation (invasive vs. non-invasive), resuscitation status, and end-of-life care.',
