      title: '+ Provide age-appropriate emotional support to an 11-year-old boy facing wheelchair transition, addressing body image, peer relationships, and self-worth.',
      content: '**Step 1 — Create a Safe Space and Validate His Feelings**:\n"Thank you for telling me that. It takes courage to say what you are feeling, and I am really glad you trust me enough to share it. Can I tell you something? What you are feeling right now — that worry about what your friends will think — that is completely normal. Any kid your age would feel the same way. You are not being weak or silly. You are being honest, and that matters."\n\n**Step 2 — Explore His Specific Fears**:\n"Can you tell me more about what you are worried about? Is it what your three close friends would think, or is it more about the other kids at school? Have any of them said anything about your walking lately?"\n[Listen actively. Let him lead the conversation. Do not rush to reassure.]\n\n**Step 3 — Reframe the Wheelchair as Empowerment, Not Loss**:\n"I want to tell you something that might sound weird, but hear me out. Right now, you are spending all of your energy just trying to walk to class and back. You told me walking is exhausting, and you have been skipping recess and birthday parties because of it. The wheelchair is not going to take something away from you. It is going to give you something back — energy. Energy to play video games with your friends at their houses. Energy to go to birthday parties. Energy to focus in class instead of being tired. It is like giving Iron Man his suit. Tony Stark is a genius without the suit, but the suit lets him do more, not less."\n\n**Step 4 — Address Peer Relationships Practically**:\n"About your friends — your three close friends already know you. They have watched you use a walker. They know who you are. I think you might be surprised by how they react. And here is an idea: what if you were the one to tell them about the wheelchair before they see it? You could even let them try driving it. A power wheelchair with a joystick and seat elevator is actually pretty cool. Some of my patients\' friends have been jealous."\n\n**Step 5 — Offer Connection with Peers Who Understand**:\n"There are also other boys your age who have gone through exactly what you are going through right now. MDA has programs and camps where you can meet them. Some of them are doing incredible things — playing adaptive sports, streaming video games, going to school and college. Would you be interested in meeting some of them? Sometimes it helps to talk to someone who actually knows what it feels like."\n\n**Step 6 — Empower His Autonomy**:\n"This is your wheelchair, your life, and your decision about how you want to handle it at school. We can work together on a plan. You get to decide who you tell, when you start using it, and how you want to introduce it. You are the boss of this."\n\n**Step 7 — Address What to Tell His Parents**:\n"I also want to check with you — is it okay if I share what you told me with your parents? They love you so much, and I think they would want to know how you are feeling. But if you want to talk to them yourself, or if you want me to help you talk to them, we can do that too."\n\n**Step 8 — Offer Ongoing Support**:\n"This is not a one-time conversation. I want you to know that every time you come see me, you can talk about this. If you are having a bad day, if someone says something hurtful, if you are feeling sad — you can call us. And if you want, we can connect you with a counselor who is really good at helping kids work through big changes like this. Not because something is wrong with you, but because everyone needs someone to talk to."\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Speak directly to the child, not through the parents\n- Use age-appropriate language and references (Marvel analogies work for this patient)\n- Validate emotions before problem-solving\n- Offer practical strategies for peer management\n- Empower autonomy and choice\n- Connect with peer mentors who share the experience\n- Offer ongoing psychological support without pathologizing normal grief\n- Consider formal psychology or counseling referral for adjustment support',
